## (root)
#c9[i5] The Anyhow library provides anyhow::Error, a trait object based error type for error handling in Rust applications.
  > README.md:9-10 #332995edd222
#c10[i3] Downcasting of anyhow errors is supported by value, by shared reference, or by mutable reference.
  > README.md:66-67 #e41e0e6ce253
#c11[i3] To enable backtraces for only errors, the RUST_LIB_BACKTRACE environment variable must be set to 1.
  > README.md:85 #e2e2fa1521f8
#c12[i4] The anyhow! macro constructs one-off anyhow::Error values with string interpolation, and bail! provides a shorthand early return.
  > README.md:111-112 #f47f319312be
#c13[i4] Using Anyhow in no_std mode requires disabling the default std feature in Cargo.toml and requires a global allocator.
  > README.md::"No-std support" #c52f3dc8d20d
#c14[i3] In Rust versions older than 1.81, no_std mode may require .map_err(Error::msg) when working with non-Anyhow error types.
  > README.md::"No-std support" #c52f3dc8d20d

